course: biology
lesson: 91
title: The aquarium: the shark tunnel and the rays
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In a big aquarium you can walk through a tunnel of thick clear acrylic, so the sea is all around you and sharks glide over your head.
explain: The sharks swim past the smaller fish without snapping at them, because the keepers feed them full meals from a long pole and a full shark is not hunting.
explain: Sharks and rays are cousins: both have skeletons of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ear, and a ray is really a shark flattened out for life on the sea floor.
explain: Look closely and you find surprises: shark skin made of tiny teeth, a shark egg case called a mermaid's purse, and a sawfish whose saw can feel electricity.
explain: Films like Jaws made people fear sharks, but the true numbers run the other way: people kill far more sharks than sharks ever kill people.
fact: Aquarium tunnels are made of acrylic many centimetres thick, strong enough to hold back millions of litres of water.
fact: Aquarium sharks are fed regular meals of fish, often from a long pole, and keepers record how much each one eats, so they mostly ignore their tankmates.
fact: The sand tiger shark looks fierce with its long jagged teeth, but it is slow and calm; the thin teeth are for gripping slippery fish, and it gulps air at the surface to help it float.
fact: Great white sharks, the sharks of Jaws, hunt fish and seals and are rarely kept in aquariums because they swim huge distances and many stopped eating or died in tanks.
fact: Sharks kill fewer than about ten people a year worldwide, while people kill tens of millions of sharks every year.
fact: Rays are flat relatives of sharks with cartilage skeletons; they hide in sand and eat shellfish and worms, and a stingray's barb on its tail is only for defence.
fact: In touch pools the rays' barbs are safely trimmed, and visitors stroke them gently with two fingers so the animals are not hurt.
fact: The manta ray is the biggest ray, with fins up to about 7 metres across, and it eats tiny floating plankton by swimming with its mouth open; plankton is everywhere in the sea, so filtering lots of it can feed a huge body.
fact: A sawfish is a ray with a long flat saw edged with tooth-like points; it swings the saw to stun fish, and the saw also feels the tiny electric signals of hidden prey.
fact: Sawfish are critically endangered, mainly because their saws tangle in fishing nets.
fact: Some sharks, like the nurse shark, can rest on the bottom and pump water over their gills, but others, like the great white, must keep swimming to breathe.
fact: Some sharks and skates lay eggs in tough cases called mermaid's purses, and the baby grows inside for months, fed by a yolk.
fact: Shark skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales called denticles, which feel smooth one way and like sandpaper the other and help water slip past.
fact: The whale shark is the biggest fish of all, up to about 18 metres long, and it filters plankton and small fish; it is a shark, not a whale, because it breathes with gills and has a cartilage skeleton.
exercise: What is the wall of an aquarium shark tunnel made of? | Thick clear acrylic | Thin ordinary window glass | Hard frozen ice | Clear plastic cling film
exercise: Why don't the sharks in the tunnel eat the other fish? | Keepers feed them full meals, so they are not hunting | Sharks never eat other fish | The other fish taste poisonous, so sharks spit them out | A glass wall separates every fish
exercise: What are a sand tiger shark's long jagged teeth for? | Gripping slippery fish | Biting through big whales | Cracking open shellfish | Scaring curious divers away
exercise: How does a sand tiger shark help itself float? | It gulps air at the surface | It eats lots of fat | It rests on the bottom all day | It flaps its fins nonstop
exercise: Why are great white sharks hardly ever kept in aquariums? | They swim huge distances and do badly in tanks | They are too dangerous for keepers | They only eat whales | They cannot live in salt water
exercise: Which is true about sharks and people? | People kill tens of millions of sharks a year | Sharks kill thousands of people a year | Sharks hunt people on purpose | Sharks and people never meet
exercise: What is a ray's skeleton made of? | Cartilage, like the bendy part of your ear | Hard bone just like ours | A shell on the outside | It has no skeleton at all
exercise: What is a stingray's barb used for? | Only for defence when it is stepped on | Spearing small fish | Digging holes in the sand | Fighting other stingrays
exercise: How should you touch a ray in a touch pool? | Gently with two fingers | Grab it with your whole hand | Lift it out of the water | Poke it hard to make it swim
exercise: What does a giant manta ray eat? | Tiny floating plankton | Big fish and young seals | Seaweed from the bottom | Crabs and spiny lobsters
exercise: What can a sawfish's saw sense? | The tiny electric signals of prey | The smell of blood miles away | The heat of the sun | The sound of boats
exercise: Why are sawfish critically endangered? | Their saws tangle in fishing nets | Sharks eat nearly all of their babies | They cannot find enough plankton | The seas are too cold for them
exercise: How can a nurse shark lie still on the bottom and still breathe? | It pumps water over its gills | It holds its breath for hours | It breathes air through its nose | It does not need oxygen
exercise: What is a mermaid's purse? | A shark or skate egg case | A spiral shell a hermit crab lives in | A ray's empty skin | A bag of shark teeth
exercise: What are the tiny tooth-like scales on shark skin called? | Denticles | Tiny feathers | Sharp barbs | Gill slits
exercise: Why is a whale shark a shark and not a whale? | It breathes with gills and has a cartilage skeleton | It is smaller than every kind of whale in the ocean | It lays eggs in mermaid's purses | It has sharp teeth for hunting
test: mate says the tunnel glass could crack any second if a shark headbutts it lol. true? | No, the acrylic is many centimetres thick and very strong | Yes, sharks often break the tunnels and the water floods in | Yes, that is why the sharks are kept hungry | No, because the tunnel is made of steel
test: What would most likely happen if the keepers stopped feeding the tank sharks? | The hungry sharks would start hunting their tankmates | The sharks would eat seaweed instead | Nothing, sharks never get hungry | The other fish would eat the sharks
test: Your cousin says a shark with that many teeth must be a killer. What is the best reply? | Teeth alone do not show danger; sand tigers are calm | Yes, the more teeth a shark has, the more dangerous it is | Yes, all sharks with long teeth attack people | No, sand tigers have no teeth at all
test: After watching Jaws, Ben thinks the sea is full of sharks hunting swimmers. What is the truth? | Sharks kill fewer than about ten people a year | Sharks kill thousands of swimmers every single year | Every beach has a great white nearby | Sharks follow boats to find people
test: In Finding Nemo, Bruce the great white shark is in a club where sharks promise not to eat fish. What is true of real great whites? | They do eat fish and seals, and they roam huge distances | They never eat fish at all, only tiny drifting plankton and krill | They live in small groups in one reef | They eat only seaweed and shellfish
test: A ray in the touch pool has no barb on its tail. Why? | Keepers trimmed it safely so visitors cannot be stung | Rays are born without barbs | It used the barb to spear fish and snapped it off on a rock | The barb only grows in the wild ocean
test: Why would a flat body help a ray? | It can hide in sand and glide along the sea floor | It can swim faster than any shark in the whole ocean | It can float on the surface to sunbathe | It can climb up rocks
test: 3 sawfish and 2 mantas cruise past, no wait, 4 mantas. how many tails is that | 7 | 5 | 14 | 9
test: A shark has five gill slits on each side. How many gill slits does it have in all? | 10 | 5 | 8 | 15
test: Stroke a shark from nose to tail and then from tail to nose. What do you feel? | Smooth one way and rough like sandpaper the other | Smooth both ways, just like stroking a wet dolphin | Slimy both ways like a frog | Spiky both ways like a hedgehog
test: Which is the biggest fish in the world? | The whale shark | The great blue whale | The great white shark | The giant manta ray
test: Why do some of the biggest sea animals eat some of the smallest food? | Plankton is everywhere, so filtering it feeds a huge body | Their teeth are too weak for bigger food | Small food is easier to see in the dark | They are too slow to catch anything else
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sharks maintained within aquarium exhibits refrain from predation upon their tankmates. | They are provided with regular and sufficient meals by the keepers | They are physically incapable of consuming fish of any appreciable size | Their tankmates are invariably venomous | They are separated from all other fish by partitions
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between sharks and rays? | Rays are flattened relatives of sharks, both possessing cartilaginous skeletons | Rays are bony fish unrelated to sharks | Rays are juvenile sharks which have not yet attained their adult form and fins | Rays are marine mammals resembling sharks
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the whale shark. | It is the largest fish and subsists upon plankton and small fish | It is a whale which hunts other sharks throughout the open oceans | It is a mammal that respires by means of lungs | It is a modest fish of under two metres
exam: It is given that the sawfish is designated critically endangered. Ascertain the principal cause thereof. | Entanglement of its saw in fishing nets | Predation upon it by manta rays | Insufficiency of plankton in its waters | Excessive warmth of the ocean surface
